Result: the Dockerized Oxia run exited successfully. Its JUnit report recorded
`tests=1`, `skipped=0`, `failures=0`, `errors=0` for
`currentHeadReadRecoversAfterRealOxiaRestartNotificationGap`.

The test observed a real current-head notification for the first publication
before restart. After stopping and restarting Oxia, a replacement publisher
published the next snapshot, and the same pre-restart subscriber authority's
`current(scope.digest())` read returned that exact publication. This verifies
the direct current-head read through the existing client after a real service
restart. The test does not assert that its notification stream resumes or
exercise the Host's bounded safety-recheck timer.

An earlier strict experiment required a post-restart notification and timed
out after 30 seconds with no resumed notification. That result is consistent
with the design contract that notifications can be lost and bounded rereads
must provide correctness. The passing test intentionally verifies the direct
head read instead of treating notification delivery as guaranteed.

The smoke uses test fixtures for publisher trust and authorization. It does
not verify the production Host timer wired to Oxia, other external-event
invalidation, production authority assembly, or centralized validation. The
Java test source was line-wrapped after this run to satisfy Checkstyle; the
current SHA-256 is
`07f7ea46db56d0857be89c12e2d8a57a3a30cb83c05bbb0a6ee432959faa4339`, with test
